bsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS3.SSS4\" title=\"In III-C Shared Attention-Based Encoder ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>4 </span>Segment states</span></a></li>\n198\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS3.SSS5\" title=\"In III-C Shared Attention-Based Encoder ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>5 </span>Asymmetric cross-attention</span></a></li>\n199\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS3.SSS6\" title=\"In III-C Shared Attention-Based Encoder ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>6 </span>Self-attention across segments</span></a></li>\n200\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS3.SSS7\" title=\"In III-C Shared Attention-Based Encoder ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>7 </span>Window embedding</span></a></li>\n201\t</ol>\n202\t</li>\n203\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\">\n204\t<a href=\"#S3.SS4\" title=\"In III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-D</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Dynamic Fusion of Window Embeddings</span></span></a>\n205\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_subsection\">\n206\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS4.SSS1\" title=\"In III-D Dynamic Fusion of Window Embeddings ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-D</span>1 </span>Concatenation</span></a></li>\n207\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS4.SSS2\" title=\"In III-D Dynamic Fusion of Window Embeddings ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-D</span>2 </span>Dynamic fusion</span></a></li>\n208\t</ol>\n209\t</li>\n210\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\">\n211\t<a href=\"#S3.SS5\" title=\"In III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-E</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Training and Augmentation</span></span></a>\n212\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_subsection\">\n213\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS5.SSS1\" title=\"In III-E Training and Augmentation ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-E</span>1 </span>Loss</span></a></li>\n214\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS5.SSS2\" title=\"In III-E Training and Augmentation ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-E</span>2 </span>Augmentation</span></a></li>\n215\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS5.SSS3\" title=\"In III-E Training and Augmentation ‣ III Methodology ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">III-E</span>3 </span>Regularization</span></a></li>\n216\t</ol>\n217\t</li>\n218\t</ol>\n219\t</li>\n220\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\">\n221\t<a href=\"#S4\" title=\"In A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">IV </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results</span></span></a>\n222\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n223\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS1\" title=\"In IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-A</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Data Collection</span></span></a></li>\n224\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS2\" title=\"In IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-B</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Two-Class Emotion Recognition</span></span></a></li>\n225\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS3\" title=\"In IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-C</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Three-Class Emotion Recognition</span></span></a></li>\n226\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS4\" title=\"In IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-D</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Computational Cost</span></span></a></li>\n227\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS5\" title=\"In IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-E</span> </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Overall Analysis &amp; Discussion</span></span></a></li>\n228\t</ol>\n229\t</li>\n230\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S5\" title=\"In A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">V </span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Conclusion</span></span></a></li>\n231\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography\"><a href=\"#bib\" title=\"In A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\">References</span></a></li>\n232\t</ol></nav>\n233\t</nav>\n234\t<div class=\"ltx_page_main\">\n235\t<div id=\"infobox\" class=\"infobox\">\n236\t  <a id=\"license-tr\" href=\"https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available\">\n237\t    License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n238\t  </a>\n239\t  <div id=\"watermark-tr\">\n240\tarXiv:2608.09088v1 [cs.AI] 10 Aug 2026</div>\n241\t</div><div class=\"ltx_page_content\">\n242\t<article class=\"ltx_document ltx_authors_1line\">\n243\t<h1 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_document\">A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\n244\t<br class=\"ltx_break\">\n245\t</h1>\n246\t<div class=\"ltx_authors\">\n247\t<span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n248\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">\n249\tStefanos Gkikas\n250\t</span></span>\n251\t<span class=\"ltx_author_before\">  </span><span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n252\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Yang Guo\n253\t</span></span>\n254\t<span class=\"ltx_author_before\">  </span><span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n255\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Guangliang Li\n256\t</span></span>\n257\t<span class=\"ltx_author_before\">  </span><span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n258\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Raul Fernandez Rojas\n259\t</span></span>\n260\t<span class=\"ltx_author_before\">  </span><span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n261\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Giorgos Giannakakis\n262\t</span></span>\n263\t<span class=\"ltx_author_before\">  </span><span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n264\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Randy Gomez\n265\t</span></span>\n266\t</div>\n267\t\n268\t<div id=\"abstract1\" class=\"ltx_abstract\">\n269\t<h6 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_abstract\">Abstract</h6>\n270\t<p class=\"ltx_p\">Mixed emotions represent a clinically relevant but still underexplored target for automatic emotion recognition. EEG provides millisecond-level access to neural activity, yet most EEG pipelines analyze the signal through a single temporal window, thereby fixing the temporal structure available to the model.\n271\tThis study introduces a multi-scale temporal framework for EEG-based emotion recognition. The EEG waveform is decomposed into windows of one or several durations, processed by a shared attention-based encoder, and integrated through a dynamic fusion module that assigns sample-specific weights across temporal scales. The framework is evaluated under a subject-independent protocol in binary and three-class settings, with the three-class task including the mixed affective category.\n272\tThe best results are <math class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"65.22\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>65.22</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">65.22\\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the two-class task and <math class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"45.43\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>45.43</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45.43\\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the three-class task. Both are obtained with three-scale dynamic-fusion configurations and remain substantially above the full-signal baseline. The best-performing temporal scales differ between the two tasks.\n273\tDynamic fusion outperforms concatenation in the highest-scoring two-class configuration and slightly exceeds it in the highest-scoring three-class configuration, although these multi-scale settings require substantially more computation than the full-signal baseline.</p>\n274\t</div>\n275\t<section id=\"S1\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n276\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n277\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">I </span><span id=\"S1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Introduction</span>\n278\t</h2>\n279\t\n280\t<div id=\"S1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n281\t<p id=\"S1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Mixed emotions refer to emotional episodes in which positive and negative affective components occur at the same time <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">larsen_mcgraw_2011</span>]</cite>. They are not adequately described by a single valence axis. Instead, they form a distinct affective category that requires the two poles to be evaluated independently <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">oh_tong_2022</span>]</cite>. Instruments such as the Positive and Negative Affect Schedule <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">watson_clark_1988</span>]</cite> and valence-arousal-dominance ratings <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">bradley_lang_1994</span>]</cite> are commonly used to assess subjective affective experience. However, reconstructing the conflicting components of a mixed affective state after the event is difficult, and retrospective self-report becomes unreliable when affective states must be monitored continuously <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">aaker_drolet_2008</span>]</cite>.</p>\n282\t</div>\n283\t<div id=\"S1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n284\t<p id=\"S1.p2.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">The global burden of mental health conditions is substantial. Estimates attribute an additional <math id=\"S1.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"53\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>53</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">53</annotation></semantics></math> million cases of major depressive disorder and <math id=\"S1.p2.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"76\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>76</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">76</annotation></semantics></math> million cases of anxiety disorder to the COVID-19 pandemic in a single year <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">santomauro_herrera_2021</span>]</cite>. Mixed affective states are clinically important because they preserve the simultaneous presence of opposing affective components, information that single-valence descriptors discard <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">larsen_mcgraw_2014</span>]</cite>. Evidence from psychotherapy populations further shows that concurrent positive and negative affect can precede improvements in psychological well-being, highlighting the value of characterizing mixed emotional experience for mental health monitoring <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">adler_hershfield_2012</span>]</cite>.</p>\n285\t</div>\n286\t<div id=\"S1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n287\t<p id=\"S1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Self-report alone is limited in settings that require continuous and unobtrusive monitoring. EEG frequency-band features have been studied across psychiatric conditions such as depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety, and post-traumatic stress disorder <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">newson_thiagarajan_2019</span>]</cite>. Wearable systems that apply machine learning to physiological signals have also shown promise for the passive detection of anxiety and depression <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">abd_alrazaq_alsaad_2023</span>]</cite>. Among physiological modalities, EEG directly measures central nervous system activity with millisecond resolution, capturing affective dynamics that peripheral biosignals cannot express in the same way <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">alarcao_fonseca_2019</span>]</cite>.</p>\n288\t</div>\n289\t<div id=\"S1.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n290\t<p id=\"S1.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Automatic emotion recognition from EEG has advanced considerably with deep learning, especially for discrete affective classification tasks <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">song_zheng_2020</span>]</cite>. Convolutional and recurrent architectures have been applied to spectral and temporal EEG representations <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">zheng_lu_2015</span>]</cite>, while attention-based models have enabled longer-range dependencies to be modeled within EEG sequences <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">song_zheng_2023</span>]</cite>. Affective responses, however, do not evolve at a single temporal resolution. They include both short, transient reactions and sustained dynamics throughout the full stimulus duration. The analysis window therefore remains a central design choice, as it determines the temporal information exposed to the model <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">alarcao_fonseca_2019</span>]</cite>. Mixed emotional states, where positive and negative components co-occur, remain comparatively underexplored from a computational perspective despite their clinical relevance <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">larsen_mcgraw_2011</span>]</cite>.</p>\n291\t</div>\n292\t<div id=\"S1.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n293\t<p id=\"S1.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">This study proposes a multi-scale temporal framework for EEG-based emotion recognition. Time-domain EEG waveforms are decomposed into windows of multiple durations, encoded by a shared attention-based model, and integrated through a dynamic fusion mechanism that assigns sample-adaptive weights across temporal scales. The framework is evaluated in binary and three-class classification settings covering positive, negative, and mixed affective categories.</p>\n294\t</div>\n295\t</section>\n296\t<section id=\"S2\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n297\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n298\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">II </span><span id=\"S2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Related Work</span>\n299\t</h2>\n300\t\n301\t<div id=\"S2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n302\t<p id=\"S2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Automatic emotion recognition has been studied across physiological and behavioral modalities, including electrodermal activity, photoplethysmography, cardiovascular signals, and facial video <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">koelstra_muhl_2012</span>]</cite>. EEG occupies a particular role in this setting because it records central nervous system activity at millisecond resolution and provides access to affective correlates that peripheral biosignals cannot directly express <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">alarcao_fonseca_2019</span>]</cite>.\n303\tRecent work in affective computing has also examined how the organization and fusion of behavioral and physiological information affect recognition performance. Examples include graph-based facial representations for stress recognition, channel-stacked visual representations of EDA for stress detection, and modality-agnostic fusion of heterogeneous physiological signals for cognitive workload assessment <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">kassiotis_stressgat_acii_2026</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_eda_stress_prai_2026</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_workload_acii_2026</span>]</cite>.</p>\n304\t</div>\n305\t<div id=\"S2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n306\t<p id=\"S2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Early EEG-based emotion recognition methods relied on hand-crafted spectral descriptors paired with shallow classifiers. Later approaches introduced deep architectures, including deep belief networks operating on differential entropy features <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">zheng_lu_2015</span>]</cite>. Differential entropy across the delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma bands has since become one of the most common feature representations for EEG emotion classification <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">duan_zhu_2013</span>]</cite>. Graph convolutional networks were then introduced to model electrode topology and learn functional relationships between channels beyond static feature representations <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">song_zheng_2020</span>]</cite>. More recently, transformer-based decoders that combine convolutional modules with self-attention have improved the modeling of local spatiotemporal patterns and long-range dependencies <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">song_zheng_2023</span>]</cite>.\n307\tRelated studies have also explored multimodal, modality-agnostic, and spatiotemporal representation learning across behavioral and physiological signal analysis <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_tsiknakis_slr_2023</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_tsiknakis_painvit_2024</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_arzate_pain_icmi_2026</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_arzate_eeite_pain_2026</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_tsiknakis_thermal_2024</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_reface_acii_2026</span>]</cite>.</p>\n308\t</div>\n309\t<div id=\"S2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n310\t<p id=\"S2.p3.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">Temporal segmentation is a key design factor in EEG-based emotion recognition. The selected analysis window determines the balance between short-term reactivity and longer affective dynamics. Studies on public benchmarks have shown that performance is strongly affected by window length. Wavelet-based features extracted from <math id=\"S2.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"3\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3</annotation></semantics></math>- to <math id=\"S2.p3.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"12\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12</annotation></semantics></math>-second segments improved arousal and valence classification compared with full-trial analysis <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">candra_yuwono_2015</span>]</cite>. Similar behavior has been reported for deep networks, where CNN performance under subject-independent evaluation varies with the temporal window and differs between arousal and valence <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">keelawat_thammasan_2021</span>]</cite>. Other studies confirm that the effective temporal resolution depends on both the dataset and the affective dimension, with optimal windows ranging from a few seconds to more than ten seconds <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">ouyang_zhou_2022</span>]</cite>.</p>\n311\t</div>\n312\t<div id=\"S2.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n313\t<p id=\"S2.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Beyond fixed-window analysis, multi-scale architectures have been developed to represent short-term and sustained EEG dynamics within a single model. Multi-scale convolutional and graph-based models encode EEG activity at multiple temporal resolutions <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">ms_imamba_2024</span>, <span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">wang_msgm_2025</span>]</cite>, based on the premise that affective processes unfold across multiple temporal scales. A related multi-window fusion strategy has also been explored for pain recognition from respiratory signals, where a single cross-attention transformer aggregates representations extracted at different temporal resolutions <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">gkikas_kyprakis_resp_2025</span>]</cite>.\n314\tUnlike that approach, which applies multi-window modeling to a single respiration signal for pain recognition, the present framework operates directly on multichannel EEG waveforms, processes variable-length windows through a shared multi-layer segment-based encoder, and uses sample-adaptive fusion to weight temporal scales and positions for each recording.\n315\tDespite these developments, most EEG-based emotion recognition studies still focus on standard discrete or dimensional categories. The co-occurrence of positive and negative affect that characterizes mixed emotional states remains largely unaddressed in computational approaches <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<span class=\"ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self\">larsen_mcgraw_2011</span>]</cite>.</p>\n316\t</div>\n317\t</section>\n318\t<section id=\"S3\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n319\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n320\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">III </span><span id=\"S3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Methodology</span>\n321\t</h2>\n322\t\n323\t<div id=\"S3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n324\t<p id=\"S3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">This section describes the proposed multi-scale temporal framework. Time-domain EEG waveforms are cropped to a common length, decomposed into windows of one or several durations, encoded by a shared attention-based model, and integrated through dynamic fusion. The training objective, augmentation procedure, and regularization settings are also presented.</p>\n325\t</div>\n326\t<section id=\"S3.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n327\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n328\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS1.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-A</span> </span><span id=\"S3.SS1.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Signal Representation</span>\n329\t</h3>\n330\t\n331\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n332\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Each EEG recording is represented as a multi-channel waveform sampled at a fixed rate <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f_{s}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. A sample consists of a tensor</p>\n333\t<table id=\"S3.E1\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n334\t\n335\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n336\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n337\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{X}_{\\mathrm{raw}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L_{0}},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{X}_{\\mathrm{raw}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L_{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n338\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n339\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(1)</span></td>\n340\t</tr></tbody>\n341\t</table>\n342\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.8\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of EEG channels and <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.3.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L_{0}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the raw signal length. A stride <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.4.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"r\\in\\mathbb{N}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\in\\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be optionally applied along the temporal axis, producing a strided signal of length <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.5.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L_{0}/r\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{0}/r</annotation></semantics></math>.\n343\tIn all experiments reported in this study, <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.6.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"r=1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>; therefore, the EEG waveform remains at its original sampling rate of <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.7.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"300\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300</annotation></semantics></math> Hz.\n344\tIn this work, no hand-crafted feature extraction is applied; the model operates directly on the waveform. All signals are cropped to a fixed target length <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.8.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> selected to match the shortest recording available in the dataset:</p>\n345\t<table id=\"S3.E2\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n346\t\n347\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n348\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n349\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L=\\left\\lfloor L_{\\min}/r\\right\\rfloor.\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\left\\lfloor L_{\\min}/r\\right\\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n350\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n351\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(2)</span></td>\n352\t</tr></tbody>\n353\t</table>\n354\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.11\" class=\"ltx_p\">During training, the crop position is uniformly sampled from the valid range <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.9.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"[0,L_{0}/r-L]\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,L_{0}/r-L]</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding multiple temporal views per epoch for recordings longer than <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.10.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>. During validation and testing, the crop position is fixed at zero, yielding a deterministic first-<math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.11.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> crop for every sample. The resulting tensor is</p>\n355\t<table id=\"S3.E3\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n356\t\n357\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n358\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n359\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L}.\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n360\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n361\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(3)</span></td>\n362\t</tr></tbody>\n363\t</table>\n364\t</div>\n365\t</section>\n366\t<section id=\"S3.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n367\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n368\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS2.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-B</span> </span><span id=\"S3.SS2.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Multi-Scale Temporal Windowing</span>\n369\t</h3>\n370\t\n371\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n372\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Given the cropped signal <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, a set of window durations is defined as</p>\n373\t<table id=\"S3.E4\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n374\t\n375\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n376\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n377\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E4.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{W}=\\{w_{1},w_{2},\\ldots,w_{K}\\},\\quad w_{k}\\in\\{\\mathrm{full},2,5,10\\}\\ \\text{seconds},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace=\"1.167em\">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>full</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0.500em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mtext>seconds</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{W}=\\{w_{1},w_{2},\\ldots,w_{K}\\},\\quad w_{k}\\in\\{\\mathrm{full},2,5,10\\}\\ \\text{seconds},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n378\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n379\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(4)</span></td>\n380\t</tr></tbody>\n381\t</table>\n382\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.5\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathrm{full}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>full</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{full}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entire cropped signal used as a single window. Each duration <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.3.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"w_{k}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> produces <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.4.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n_{k}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> non-overlapping windows of length <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.5.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\ell_{k}=w_{k}f_{s}/r\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_{k}=w_{k}f_{s}/r</annotation></semantics></math> samples,</p>\n383\t<table id=\"S3.E5\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n384\t\n385\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n386\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n387\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E5.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n_{k}=\\left\\lfloor L/\\ell_{k}\\right\\rfloor,\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{k}=\\left\\lfloor L/\\ell_{k}\\right\\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n388\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n389\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(5)</span></td>\n390\t</tr></tbody>\n391\t</table>\n392\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.10\" class=\"ltx_p\">so that the total number of windows extracted per sample is</p>\n393\t<table id=\"S3.E6\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n394\t\n395\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n396\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n397\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E6.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"N=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}n_{k}.\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace=\"0.111em\">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits=\"false\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}n_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n398\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n399\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(6)</span></td>\n400\t</tr></tbody>\n401\t</table>\n402\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.8\" class=\"ltx_p\">For <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.6.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"w_{k}=\\mathrm{full}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>full</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{k}=\\mathrm{full}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.7.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\ell_{k}=L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_{k}=L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.8.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n_{k}=1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each window is a tensor</p>\n403\t<table id=\"S3.E7\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n404\t\n405\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n406\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n407\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E7.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{X}^{(k,i)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times\\ell_{k}},\\quad i=1,\\ldots,n_{k},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace=\"1.167em\">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{X}^{(k,i)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times\\ell_{k}},\\quad i=1,\\ldots,n_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n408\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n409\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(7)</span></td>\n410\t</tr></tbody>\n411\t</table>\n412\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.9\" class=\"ltx_p\">containing all <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.9.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> EEG channels within its temporal interval.</p>\n413\t</div>\n414\t</section>\n415\t<section id=\"S3.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n416\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n417\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span> </span><span id=\"S3.SS3.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Shared Attention-Based Encoder</span>\n418\t</h3>\n419\t\n420\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n421\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">All <math id=\"S3.SS3.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"N\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> windows are processed by a single shared encoder. Weight sharing across scales enables the model to learn a common representation function regardless of window duration, while keeping the encoder parameter count independent of <math id=\"S3.SS3.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"N\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>; only the lightweight fusion head and the window-specific normalization layers vary with the window configuration. Before encoding, each window is normalized through a window-length-specific layer normalization module.</p>\n422\t</div>\n423\t<section id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n424\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\">\n425\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>1 </span>Tokenization</h4>\n426\t\n427\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n428\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.6\" class=\"ltx_p\">Each window <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{X}^{(k,i)}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{X}^{(k,i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a 1D signal with <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> channels and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\ell_{k}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> temporal positions. The temporal axis is flattened into a sequence of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\ell_{k}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, each carrying the <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> channel values at the corresponding time step. Geometric information is incorporated by encoding each normalized time position <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"t\\in[-1,1]\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with Fourier features:</p>\n429\t<table id=\"S3.E8\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n430\t\n431\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n432\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n433\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E8.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\begin{split}\\gamma(t)=\\bigl[&amp;\\sin(\\pi s_{1}t),\\ \\cos(\\pi s_{1}t),\\ \\ldots,\\\\\n434\t&amp;\\sin(\\pi s_{M}t),\\ \\cos(\\pi s_{M}t),\\ t\\bigr],\\end{split}\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mtable columnspacing=\"0pt\" displaystyle=\"true\" rowspacing=\"0pt\"><mtr><mtd class=\"ltx_align_right\" columnalign=\"right\"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">[</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"ltx_align_left\" columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.667em\">,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.667em\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class=\"ltx_align_left\" columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.667em\">,</mo><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.667em\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{split}\\gamma(t)=\\bigl[&amp;\\sin(\\pi s_{1}t),\\ \\cos(\\pi s_{1}t),\\ \\ldots,\\\\\n435\t&amp;\\sin(\\pi s_{M}t),\\ \\cos(\\pi s_{M}t),\\ t\\bigr],\\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n436\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n437\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(8)</span></td>\n438\t</tr></tbody>\n439\t</table>\n440\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.11\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.7.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\{s_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{M}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> span <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.8.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"[1,f_{\\max}/2]\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[1,f_{\\max}/2]</annotation></semantics></math>. The model uses <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.9.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"M=6\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=6</annotation></semantics></math> frequency bands and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.10.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f_{\\max}=10\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\max}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, adding <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.11.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"2M+1=13\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2M+1=13</annotation></semantics></math> positional features per token. Channel values and positional features are concatenated at each step to form the token matrix</p>\n441\t<table id=\"S3.E9\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n442\t\n443\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n444\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n445\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E9.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{T}^{(k,i)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell_{k}\\times C^{\\prime}},\\quad C^{\\prime}=C+(2M+1).\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace=\"1.167em\">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{T}^{(k,i)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell_{k}\\times C^{\\prime}},\\quad C^{\\prime}=C+(2M+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n446\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n447\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(9)</span></td>\n448\t</tr></tbody>\n449\t</table>\n450\t</div>\n451\t</section>\n452\t<section id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n453\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\">\n454\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>2 </span>Segmentation</h4>\n455\t\n456\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n457\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.23\" class=\"ltx_p\">The token sequence is partitioned into <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"S=100\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=100</annotation></semantics></math> contiguous segments of length <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n_{s}=\\lceil\\ell_{k}/S\\rceil\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{s}=\\lceil\\ell_{k}/S\\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, padded when necessary. The tokens of segment <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"s\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted\n458\t<math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\tilde{\\mathbf{T}}^{(k,i)}_{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{s}\\times C^{\\prime}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde{\\mathbf{T}}^{(k,i)}_{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{s}\\times C^{\\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.\n459\tWith <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"r=1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"2\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2</annotation></semantics></math>-, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.7.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"5\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5</annotation></semantics></math>-, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.8.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"10\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10</annotation></semantics></math>-, and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.9.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"20\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20</annotation></semantics></math>-second inputs contain <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.10.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"600\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>600</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.11.m11\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"1500\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>1500</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1500</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.12.m12\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"3000\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>3000</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3000</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.13.m13\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"6000\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>6000</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6000</annotation></semantics></math> samples, respectively. Using <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.14.m14\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"S=100\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=100</annotation></semantics></math> therefore produces segments of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.15.m15\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"6\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.16.m16\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"15\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.17.m17\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"30\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.18.m18\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"60\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60</annotation></semantics></math> samples, corresponding to local intervals of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.19.m19\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"20\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.20.m20\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"50\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.21.m21\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"100\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.22.m22\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"200\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200</annotation></semantics></math> ms. Cross-attention performs learned aggregation within each interval rather than fixed averaging, while self-attention across the resulting <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.23.m23\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"100\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math> segment states models dependencies over the complete window.</p>\n460\t</div>\n461\t</section>\n462\t<section id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n463\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\">\n464\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>3 </span>Hierarchical block schedule</h4>\n465\t\n466\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n467\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The encoder consists of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"D=6\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=6</annotation></semantics></math> layers with progressively compressed latent representations. The per-layer latent dimensionality is</p>\n468\t<table id=\"S3.E10\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n469\t\n470\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n471\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n472\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E10.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"d_{\\ell}\\in\\{128,\\ 128,\\ 96,\\ 96,\\ 64,\\ 32\\},\\quad\\ell=1,\\ldots,6,\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 96</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 96</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 32</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace=\"1.167em\">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_{\\ell}\\in\\{128,\\ 128,\\ 96,\\ 96,\\ 64,\\ 32\\},\\quad\\ell=1,\\ldots,6,</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n473\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n474\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(10)</span></td>\n475\t</tr></tbody>\n476\t</table>\n477\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.7\" class=\"ltx_p\">while the number of segment states is fixed at <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"S=100\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=100</annotation></semantics></math> across all layers. Cross-attention uses <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.3.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\{8,8,6,6,4,2\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{8,8,6,6,4,2\\}</annotation></semantics></math> heads across the six layers with a head dimension of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.4.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"16\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16</annotation></semantics></math> in every layer. Self-attention uses the same head schedule and head dimension. The resulting attention inner dimensionalities are <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.5.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\{128,128,96,96,64,32\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>96</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>96</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{128,128,96,96,64,32\\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each cross-attention layer is followed by <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.6.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"R_{\\ell}\\in\\{8,8,4,4,2,1\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\ell}\\in\\{8,8,4,4,2,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math> self-attention blocks. Attention and feedforward dropout are fixed at <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.7.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.20\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.20</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20</annotation></semantics></math> across all layers.</p>\n478\t</div>\n479\t</section>\n480\t<section id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n481\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\">\n482\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>4 </span>Segment states</h4>\n483\t\n484\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n485\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">A single latent seed is derived at runtime from a set of <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"M_{0}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable global parameters <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\{\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{M_{0}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi class=\"ltx_mathvariant_bold\" mathvariant=\"bold\">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{M_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>\n486\t<table id=\"S3.E11\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n487\t\n488\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n489\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n490\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E11.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{init}}=\\frac{1}{M_{0}}\\sum_{m=1}^{M_{0}}\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class=\"ltx_mathvariant_bold\" mathvariant=\"bold\">ℓ</mi><mi>init</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits=\"false\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></munderover><msub><mi class=\"ltx_mathvariant_bold\" mathvariant=\"bold\">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{init}}=\\frac{1}{M_{0}}\\sum_{m=1}^{M_{0}}\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n491\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n492\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(11)</span></td>\n493\t</tr></tbody>\n494\t</table>\n495\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.3\" class=\"ltx_p\">and replicated across all <math id=\"S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"S\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> segments to instantiate the initial segment states. Segment-specific representations are not learned directly; they emerge through the attention updates.</p>\n496\t</div>\n497\t</section>\n498\t<section id=\"S3.SS3.SSS5\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n499\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\">\n500\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\"><span id=\"S3.SS3.SSS5.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">III-C</span>5 </span>Asymmetric cross-attention</h4>\n501\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3a177886-a8b9-499f-ad21-dfd988c51699", "uuid": "6b3812d6-7385-4be8-95b4-42400f1e628a", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T05:39:27.177Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/3a177886-a8b9-499f-ad21-dfd988c51699/tool-results/b3hp0k610.txt", "content": "<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<title>A Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic Fusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition</title>\n<!--Generated on Mon Aug 10 03:35:40 2026 by LaTeXML (version 0.8.8) http://dlmf.nist.gov/LaTeXML/.-->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no\">\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/css/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.css\" type=\"text/css\">\n<script src=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/js/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.js\"></script>\n<script>\n  // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or\n  // enact the browser preference if \"automatic\", \n  // without expecting DOM load to have completed.\n  //\n  // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences.\n  function initializeR