{n_{s}\\times C^{\\prime}} . With r = 1 r=1 , the 2 2 -, 5 5 -, 10 10 -, and 20 20 -second inputs contain 600 600 , 1500 1500 , 3000 3000 , and 6000 6000 samples, respectively. Using S = 100 S=100 therefore produces segments of 6 6 , 15 15 , 30 30 , and 60 60 samples, corresponding to local intervals of 20 20 , 50 50 , 100 100 , and 200 200 ms. Cross-attention performs learned aggregation within each interval rather than fixed averaging, while self-attention across the resulting 100 100 segment states models dependencies over the complete window. III-C 3 Hierarchical block schedule The encoder consists of D = 6 D=6 layers with progressively compressed latent representations. The per-layer latent dimensionality is d ℓ ∈ { 128 , 128 , 96 , 96 , 64 , 32 } , ℓ = 1 , … , 6 , d_{\\ell}\\in\\{128,\\ 128,\\ 96,\\ 96,\\ 64,\\ 32\\},\\quad\\ell=1,\\ldots,6, (10) while the number of segment states is fixed at S = 100 S=100 across all layers. Cross-attention uses { 8 , 8 , 6 , 6 , 4 , 2 } \\{8,8,6,6,4,2\\} heads across the six layers with a head dimension of 16 16 in every layer. Self-attention uses the same head schedule and head dimension. The resulting attention inner dimensionalities are { 128 , 128 , 96 , 96 , 64 , 32 } \\{128,128,96,96,64,32\\} . Each cross-attention layer is followed by R ℓ ∈ { 8 , 8 , 4 , 4 , 2 , 1 } R_{\\ell}\\in\\{8,8,4,4,2,1\\} self-attention blocks. Attention and feedforward dropout are fixed at 0.20 0.20 across all layers. III-C 4 Segment states A single latent seed is derived at runtime from a set of M 0 M_{0} learnable global parameters { ℓ m } m = 1 M 0 \\{\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{M_{0}} : ℓ init = 1 M 0 ​ ∑ m = 1 M 0 ℓ m ∈ ℝ d 1 , \\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{init}}=\\frac{1}{M_{0}}\\sum_{m=1}^{M_{0}}\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}, (11) and replicated across all S S segments to instantiate the initial segment states. Segment-specific representations are not learned directly; they emerge through the attention updates. III-C 5 Asymmetric cross-attention At each layer ℓ \\ell , every segment state aggregates information exclusively from its corresponding token subset: 𝐮 s ( ℓ ) = 𝐮 s ( ℓ − 1 ) + Attn ​ ( 𝐮 s ( ℓ − 1 ) , 𝐓 ~ s ( k , i ) ) , \\mathbf{u}_{s}^{(\\ell)}=\\mathbf{u}_{s}^{(\\ell-1)}+\\mathrm{Attn}\\bigl(\\mathbf{u}_{s}^{(\\ell-1)},\\tilde{\\mathbf{T}}^{(k,i)}_{s}\\bigr), (12) where 𝐮 s ( ℓ − 1 ) ∈ ℝ 1 × d ℓ \\mathbf{u}_{s}^{(\\ell-1)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times d_{\\ell}} provides the query and 𝐓 ~ s ( k , i ) ∈ ℝ n s × C ′ \\tilde{\\mathbf{T}}^{(k,i)}_{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{s}\\times C^{\\prime}} provides the keys and values. The operation is asymmetric: the query side is a single vector while the key-value side spans n s ≫ 1 n_{s}\\gg 1 tokens of dimension C ′ C^{\\prime} , and the resulting attention matrix is 1 × n s 1\\times n_{s} rather than square. All S S segments are processed in parallel by packing them into the batch dimension. III-C 6 Self-attention across segments After cross-attention, the segment states are stacked as 𝐔 ( ℓ ) ∈ ℝ S × d ℓ \\mathbf{U}^{(\\ell)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{S\\times d_{\\ell}} and refined through R ℓ R_{\\ell} self-attention blocks with pre-layer normalization and residual connections: 𝐔 ( ℓ ) ← 𝐔 ( ℓ ) + Attn ​ ( 𝐔 ( ℓ ) , 𝐔 ( ℓ ) ) . \\mathbf{U}^{(\\ell)}\\leftarrow\\mathbf{U}^{(\\ell)}+\\mathrm{Attn}\\bigl(\\mathbf{U}^{(\\ell)},\\mathbf{U}^{(\\ell)}\\bigr). (13) Between consecutive layers, the segment representations are projected from d ℓ d_{\\ell} to d ℓ + 1 d_{\\ell+1} through a linear transformation when the dimensionality changes. III-C 7 Window embedding After the final layer, the S S segment states are averaged across the segment axis to produce a single embedding per window: 𝐞 ( k , i ) = 1 S ​ ∑ s = 1 S 𝐔 s ( D ) ∈ ℝ d , \\mathbf{e}^{(k,i)}=\\frac{1}{S}\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\mathbf{U}^{(D)}_{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}, (14) where d = d D = 32 d=d_{D}=32 . All windows are represented in the same embedding space ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} independently of their duration, since the encoder is shared across scales. III-D Dynamic Fusion of Window Embeddings The N N window embeddings { 𝐞 ( k , i ) } \\{\\mathbf{e}^{(k,i)}\\} produced by the shared encoder are collected into a matrix 𝐙 = [ 𝐞 1 ; 𝐞 2 ; … ; 𝐞 N ] ∈ ℝ N × d \\mathbf{Z}=[\\mathbf{e}_{1};\\,\\mathbf{e}_{2};\\,\\ldots;\\,\\mathbf{e}_{N}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times d} (15) and integrated by a fusion module to produce a single sample-level representation for classification. Two fusion strategies are considered. III-D 1 Concatenation The embeddings are concatenated along the channel dimension and passed through a two-layer MLP with layer normalization, GELU activation, and dropout: 𝐄 = concat ​ ( 𝐞 ( 1 , 1 ) , … , 𝐞 ( K , n K ) ) ∈ ℝ N ​ d , \\mathbf{E}=\\mathrm{concat}\\bigl(\\mathbf{e}^{(1,1)},\\ldots,\\mathbf{e}^{(K,n_{K})}\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{Nd}, (16) 𝐲 ^ = MLP ​ ( 𝐄 ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=\\mathrm{MLP}(\\mathbf{E}). (17) Under this scheme, the model has no explicit sample-adaptive weighting before the classification head; the window ordering is fixed for every sample. III-D 2 Dynamic fusion A learnable query vector 𝐪 ∈ ℝ d \\mathbf{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} and a set of learnable per-slot position embeddings { 𝐩 i } i = 1 N \\{\\mathbf{p}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N} , 𝐩 i ∈ ℝ d \\mathbf{p}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} are introduced. Attention scores are computed by shifting each embedding with its position and taking a scaled dot product with the query: α i = ( 𝐞 i + 𝐩 i ) ⊤ ​ 𝐪 d , i = 1 , … , N , \\alpha_{i}=\\frac{(\\mathbf{e}_{i}+\\mathbf{p}_{i})^{\\top}\\mathbf{q}}{\\sqrt{d}},\\quad i=1,\\ldots,N, (18) and normalized across the N N windows through a softmax: w i = exp ⁡ ( α i ) ∑ j = 1 N exp ⁡ ( α j ) . w_{i}=\\frac{\\exp(\\alpha_{i})}{\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\exp(\\alpha_{j})}. (19) The weighted window embeddings are then concatenated to form the fused representation: 𝐄 = concat ​ ( w 1 ​ 𝐞 1 , w 2 ​ 𝐞 2 , … , w N ​ 𝐞 N ) ∈ ℝ N ​ d . \\mathbf{E}=\\mathrm{concat}\\bigl(w_{1}\\mathbf{e}_{1},w_{2}\\mathbf{e}_{2},\\ldots,w_{N}\\mathbf{e}_{N}\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{Nd}. (20) 𝐄 \\mathbf{E} is passed through the same MLP head as in the concatenation strategy. The weights { w i } \\{w_{i}\\} are sample-specific, so the model can assign different importance to different temporal scales and positions depending on the content of each recording. An overview of the framework, for the { full , 2 , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,10\\} configuration, is shown in Fig. 1 . Figure 1 : Overview of the proposed multi-scale temporal framework, illustrated with the { full , 2 , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,10\\} configuration. The 18 18 -channel EEG signal of length 6000 6000 samples is decomposed into three temporal scales: one full-signal window ( 18 × 6000 18\\times 6000 ), two 10 10 -second windows ( 18 × 3000 18\\times 3000 each), and ten 2 2 -second windows ( 18 × 600 18\\times 600 each), for a total of N = 13 N=13 windows. All windows are processed by the same shared encoder into 32 32 -dimensional embeddings 𝐞 1 , … , 𝐞 13 \\mathbf{e}_{1},\\ldots,\\mathbf{e}_{13} . The embeddings are integrated by the dynamic fusion module into a single sample-level representation 𝐄 ∈ ℝ 416 \\mathbf{E}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{416} , which is passed to the classification head. III-E Training and Augmentation The model is trained for 200 200 epochs using the AdamW optimizer with a base learning rate of 10 − 4 10^{-4} , a weight decay of 0.05 0.05 , and a cosine learning-rate schedule with a 20 20 -epoch warmup from 10 − 6 10^{-6} and a minimum learning rate of 10 − 5 10^{-5} . The batch size is 32 32 for all splits. Mixed-precision training is used throughout. All experiments were conducted with a fixed random seed of 3407 3407 using seed_everything . Deterministic cuDNN execution was enabled, while cuDNN benchmarking was disabled. III-E 1 Loss The three-class task uses weighted cross-entropy with class weights [ 0.83 , 1.18 , 1.09 ] [0.83,1.18,1.09] for the mixed, negative, and positive classes, respectively, applied to account for the class imbalance in the training set. The two-class task uses standard cross-entropy, excluding the mixed class. Label smoothing with a smoothing rate of 0.15 0.15 is applied during training and disabled during evaluation. III-E 2 Augmentation All augmentations operate on the full cropped signal 𝐗 ∈ ℝ C × L \\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L} prior to window extraction, with synchronized decisions across all channels to preserve the inter-channel amplitude relationships and temporal synchrony that carry affective information. Two augmentations are applied stochastically during training. Additive noise draws a per-sample decision with probability p n ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0.50 , 0.80 ) p_{n}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0.50,0.80) and a signal-to-noise scaling factor ϕ ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 1 , 1000 ) \\phi\\sim\\mathcal{U}(1,1000) ; independent noise realizations are generated per channel at the same signal-to-noise ratio. Temporal masking draws a per-sample decision with probability p m ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0.50 , 0.80 ) p_{m}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0.50,0.80) and a masked fraction ρ ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0.15 , 0.30 ) \\rho\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0.15,0.30) ; the same temporal mask is broadcast to all C C channels, with the masked interval placed at the start, end, or center of the signal with equal probability. III-E 3 Regularization Dropout with probability 0.20 0.20 is applied in the attention and feedforward sublayers of every encoder block, and the fusion head uses a dropout of 0.20 0.20 before the final linear projection. IV Experimental Evaluation &amp; Results The proposed framework is evaluated on two tasks: binary classification of affective states as positive or negative and three-way classification as positive, negative, or mixed. Validation performance is reported using balanced accuracy, macro-averaged precision, and macro-averaged F1 score. Test performance is reported using balanced accuracy. The test set is used only for final reporting; model checkpoints and hyperparameters are selected on the validation set, and results are reported for all evaluated configurations. IV-A Data Collection The experiments use the DMER dataset [ yang_liu_2024 ] . The dataset includes 73 73 participants aged 18 18 to 35 35 (mean age 23.06 23.06 , SD 3.37 3.37 ); data from 7 7 of the original 80 80 participants were excluded because of physiological signal quality issues. Participants watched 32 32 short video clips selected from the Stanford film library through rule-based filtering and expert evaluation. The clips were designed to elicit positive, negative, and mixed emotional states: 8 8 clips for positive emotions, 8 8 for negative emotions, and 16 16 for mixed emotions. Mixed emotion is defined as the simultaneous co-occurrence of positive and negative affect and was validated through PANAS and valence-arousal-dominance self-reports. EEG was recorded with the DSI-24 wireless dry-electrode system at 300 300 Hz across 21 21 channels following the international 10 10 – 20 20 system. This study uses EEG as the input modality. The time-aligned preprocessed EEG signals provided by DMER are used directly. The dataset authors applied independent component analysis, bandpass filtering from 1 1 to 50 50 Hz, 50 50 Hz notch filtering, baseline removal, and re-referencing to Pz with ear electrodes excluded, resulting in 18 18 channels per trial. Recordings have two durations, 20 20 seconds and 30 30 seconds, and these lengths are not uniformly distributed across affective categories. To prevent duration from becoming a predictive cue, all recordings are cropped to 20 20 seconds. During training, the crop position is sampled uniformly from the available range. During validation and testing, the first 20 20 seconds are used. The three-class task classifies affective states as positive, negative, or mixed. The two-class task distinguishes positive from negative affective states, with the mixed class excluded. Subjects are split at the participant level, so no individual appears in more than one set. To reduce performance inflation due to subject-difficulty imbalance, a stratified split protocol is used. Leave-one-subject-out cross-validation under the three-class task first estimates per-subject classification difficulty. Subjects are then ranked by their combined z-score and assigned to four quartile-based groups. This ranking is used only to construct a balanced subject partition and does not influence model training, checkpoint selection, or hyperparameter tuning. The final split contains 48 48 training, 12 12 validation, and 13 13 testing subjects, with each set containing subjects from all four difficulty groups. The exact partition is reported in Table I for reproducibility. TABLE I : Subject-level split by difficulty group. Subjects are ranked by combined z-score and assigned to four quartile-based groups (Q1 = hardest, Q4 = easiest). Split Difficulty Group Q1 – Hard Q2 – Med-Hard Q3 – Med-Easy Q4 – Easy Training (48) 8, 12, 20, 25, 36, 37, 43, 45, 56, 60, 66, 71 14, 30, 33, 35, 39, 50, 51, 53, 55, 65, 72, 73 5, 6, 9, 10, 15, 23, 32, 52, 54, 59, 70, 78 11, 24, 26, 34, 40, 42, 49, 61, 63, 64, 68, 76 Validation (12) 19, 48, 58 22, 41, 79 29, 47, 77 62, 67, 69 Testing (13) 28, 74, 75, 80 21, 38, 57 1, 2, 7 18, 44, 46 • Q1: z &lt; − 0.36 z&lt;-0.36 ; Q2: − 0.36 ≤ z &lt; − 0.08 -0.36\\leq z&lt;-0.08 ; Q3: − 0.08 ≤ z &lt; 0.17 -0.08\\leq z&lt;0.17 ; Q4: z ≥ 0.17 z\\geq 0.17 . IV-B Two-Class Emotion Recognition Table II reports the results for the two-class task across all window configurations and fusion strategies. Test accuracy ranges from 56.64 % 56.64\\% to 65.22 % 65.22\\% across the 29 29 evaluated configurations. The full-signal baseline, which processes the entire cropped signal as a single window without fusion, reaches 57.41 % 57.41\\% test accuracy. Most windowed configurations improve on this baseline, indicating that shorter temporal segments provide discriminative information not fully captured by the full-signal representation. For single-scale settings, the strongest results appear at the 2 2 -second scale for both concatenation and dynamic fusion ( 63.75 % 63.75\\% and 63.26 % 63.26\\% , respectively). The 5 5 -second scale also performs well under concatenation ( 63.76 % 63.76\\% ), while dynamic fusion reaches 60.40 % 60.40\\% . At the 10 10 -second scale, concatenation achieves 60.33 % 60.33\\% and dynamic fusion reaches 61.27 % 61.27\\% . Combining multiple temporal scales further improves performance in several cases: concatenation reaches 62.75 % 62.75\\% with { full , 2 , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,10\\} , and dynamic fusion reaches 64.20 % 64.20\\% with { full , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},10\\} . The best results are obtained with dynamic fusion on three-scale configurations that combine the full signal with short and longer windows. The { full , 2 , 5 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,5\\} configuration reaches 65.19 % 65.19\\% , while { full , 2 , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,10\\} reaches 65.22 % 65.22\\% , the highest accuracy in the two-class task. The corresponding concatenation configurations remain lower, at 59.84 % 59.84\\% and 62.75 % 62.75\\% . Adding all four scales { full , 2 , 5 , 10 } \\{\\mathrm{full},2,5,10\\} does not improve performance, reaching 57.49 % 57.49\\% with concatenation and 61.82 % 61.82\\% with dynamic fusion. Dynamic fusion is therefore most useful when applied to a compact set of complementary temporal scales rather than to the full redundant scale grid. TABLE II : Two-class classification performance (positive vs. negative) across all window configurations and fusion strategies. Input Validation Testing Windows (s) Fusion Accuracy Precision F1 Accuracy full – 55.73 55.81 55.58 57.41 2 concat 58.33 58.35 58.32 63.75 5 concat 59.90 59.91 59.89 63.76 10 concat 56.77 56.99 56.43 60.33 full, 2 concat 55.73 55.91 55.38 60.87 full, 5 concat 57.29 58.20 56.07 62.29 full, 10 concat 56.25 56.25 56.25 57.39 2, 5 concat 58.85 58.86 58.85 60.89 2, 10 concat